  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·06n</p>
    <h1>Thick-billed Raven Nesting: Breeding and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Thick-billed Raven (<em>Corvus crassirostris</em>) nests on cliff faces and rocky outcrops in the Ethiopian and Eritrean highlands. The nest is a large stick structure lined with softer materials, placed on a ledge or in a cliff crevice. Clutch size is typically 3–5 eggs; incubation is approximately 20–21 days. Breeding season runs from roughly January to June, peaking in February–March.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Site</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Thick-billed Raven is an obligate cliff-nester in the great majority of records. Nest sites include:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ledges on steep cliff faces, particularly in highland gorges (Simien Mountains, Blue Nile gorge, Bale Mountains).</li>
      <li>Cavities and crevices in rock faces providing protection from above and from predators.</li>
      <li>Occasionally on large buildings, bridges, or pylons in areas with high human density and limited natural cliff sites.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pairs often return to the same nest site in successive years, adding material and enlarging the structure over time. Old nests can become very large. Nest sites with long records of occupation suggest strong site fidelity in established pairs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a large platform of sticks and branches, lined with softer materials including wool, hair, grass, and man-made fibres from human settlements. Construction is by both birds. The outer structure may be 50–80 cm across in well-established nests. The cup is relatively deep, sheltering eggs and chicks from cliff winds.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Eggs and Incubation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Clutch size: 3–5 eggs. Eggs are pale blue-green to greenish-white, with brown and grey blotching and streaking — typical large corvid egg patterning. Incubation: approximately 20–21 days, primarily by the female while the male provides food. Chicks are altricial (helpless at hatching) and are brooded and fed by both parents. Fledging occurs at approximately 6 weeks.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> In the Ethiopian Highlands (the core of the species' range), breeding is documented between approximately January and June, with peak egg-laying in February–March. This places breeding in the highland dry season before the main rains begin. Breeding timing in Eritrea and other parts of the range may vary somewhat with local conditions.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
